% dossier-function: The 2024 CBC Canadian Music Class Challenge Indigenous-language song. In spring 2024 Shawnee Kish and her sister, Mohawk language advocate Kristi Talbot, held a songwriting workshop for the Indigenous youth of their community in Fort Erie, Ontario; the guide says the participants created the song together, and that it "focuses on empowering our next generation to feel proud of the strength and power within themselves as they walk the good road towards a better future." Search summaries of the CBC article say the title came from teachings Talbot shared in the workshop (to confirm).
% dossier-elements: Not transcribed here; read the key, range and form from the lead sheet. The song is in Mohawk and English (lyrics in both are linked from the guide).
% dossier-practice: Learn the Mohawk from Shawnee Kish and Kristi Talbot's teaching video, not by ear from a recording. The guide's supplements (Language Pronunciation, Language Composition) are built on this song and suit the lesson.
% dossier-accompaniment: The guide (p. 6) asks that traditional Indigenous instruments be left to Indigenous people: "Our instruments are considered sacred" and, if an educator wishes to include them, "it is highly recommended to reserve that role for an Indigenous person, whether that be a student that is knowledgeable on the matter or bringing in a Knowledge Keeper from your community." The Band plays nothing for this record; no hand drum, rattle or powwow drum from us.
% dossier-lyrics: The words are the songwriters' and the youth workshop's, in Mohawk and English; not copied here. A Mohawk speaker's teaching (the video) is the source for pronunciation.
% dossier-honesty: Composed and owned: a 2024 song by living songwriters, not traditional. Nothing here is ours.
% dossier-licence: Te Credit wording to use: "Yohahí:yo (The Good Road)" by Shawnee Kish, with Kristi Talbot and the Indigenous youth of Fort Erie, Ontario; the 2024 CBC Canadian Music Class Challenge Indigenous-language song (CBC Music and MusiCounts) (to confirm with the songwriters).
% dossier-respect: A contemporary song written with Indigenous youth to be shared with classes; its message is the youth's own. Tell where it came from before singing it, credit the songwriters and the youth, and leave Indigenous instruments to Indigenous people.
% dossier-review: Shawnee Kish and Kristi Talbot for the credit and any posting; the TDSB Urban Indigenous Education Centre for classroom use. Neither has reviewed it.
% lesson: Before the lesson: open the lead sheet linked from the guide (good-road-sheet-music.tiiny.site) and the two videos; check the range against your class.
% lesson: Tell the story first: in spring 2024 Shawnee Kish, a Mohawk singer-songwriter, and her sister Kristi Talbot, a Mohawk language advocate and early childhood educator, held a songwriting workshop with the Indigenous youth of their community in Fort Erie, Ontario. The youth made this song together; it is about being proud of the strength in yourself as you walk the good road.
% lesson: Watch "CBC Music Class Challenge with Kristi Talbot and Shawnee Kish" (MusiCounts) for what it means to sing an Indigenous language at school.
% lesson: Pronunciation: learn the Mohawk from Shawnee Kish's teaching video, phrase by phrase, repeating after her. The guide's supplements Language Pronunciation and Language Composition (K-12) are built on Mohawk and suit this step.
% lesson: Sing from the lead sheet: the Mohawk lines with the video until they are secure, then the whole song.
% lesson: Accompaniment: none from Indigenous instruments (the guide, p. 6, asks that hand drums, rattles and the big drum be left to an Indigenous student or Knowledge Keeper). If the class wants accompaniment, use only what the printed score gives; the Band stays silent.
% lesson: Older grades: the guide's Truth and Reconciliation supplement (Grades 7-12) and Language, Music and Social Justice (K-12) can follow this song.
% lesson: Close: name the songwriters and the year again, and ask what the class noticed about the language. Do not post or share a recording of the class beyond the school; the terms are "educational purposes only".
